WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.804
Biology's most talked-about tool of this century

2
00:00:02.804 --> 00:00:04.544
was borrowed from bacteria.

3
00:00:04.944 --> 00:00:06.477
When a virus attacks,

4
00:00:06.477 --> 00:00:08.009
some bacteria save a piece of its

5
00:00:08.009 --> 00:00:08.961
DNA.

6
00:00:08.961 --> 00:00:09.727
If it returns,

7
00:00:09.727 --> 00:00:11.910
they make RNA that matches it,

8
00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.000
and an enzyme called Cas9 cuts the

9
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.229
virus apart.

10
00:00:15.628 --> 00:00:17.776
Scientists copied the trick.

11
00:00:17.776 --> 00:00:19.448
They write a guide RNA for the

12
00:00:19.448 --> 00:00:20.493
gene they want,

13
00:00:20.493 --> 00:00:21.491
Cas9 cuts there,

14
00:00:21.491 --> 00:00:23.592
and the cell's own repair system adds,

15
00:00:23.592 --> 00:00:25.634
deletes or replaces DNA.

16
00:00:26.034 --> 00:00:27.915
That makes CRISPR simpler,

17
00:00:27.915 --> 00:00:31.282
faster and cheaper than older gene-editing methods,

18
00:00:31.282 --> 00:00:32.650
and more precise.

19
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.546
It is being tested against inherited diseases

20
00:00:35.546 --> 00:00:37.613
such as sickle cell disease,

21
00:00:37.613 --> 00:00:40.144
haemophilia and cystic fibrosis.

22
00:00:40.144 --> 00:00:41.711
Sickle cell is a big concern in

23
00:00:41.711 --> 00:00:42.917
parts of India.

24
00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:45.929
Most work edits ordinary body cells,

25
00:00:45.929 --> 00:00:48.600
which affects only that person.

26
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.421
Editing eggs, sperm or embryos would pass

27
00:00:51.421 --> 00:00:53.394
changes to future generations,

28
00:00:53.394 --> 00:00:56.109
and that raises hard ethical questions.

29
00:00:56.509 --> 00:00:57.937
For Prelims and GS Three,

30
00:00:57.937 --> 00:00:59.110
remember the pair:

31
00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:01.211
the guide RNA finds the spot,

32
00:01:01.211 --> 00:01:02.986
and Cas9 cuts it.

